NEPALI CHAT STYLE SPEC
The single source of truth for Sahayak's Nepali conversational register.
style_spec_version: 1

WHAT THIS FILE IS
This artifact (tools/train-intent/seeds/nepali-chat-style-spec) is THE definition of the
conversational register for chat replies, in Nepali, to an elderly speaker. Every consumer
resolves against it and no consumer re-authors a rule:
  - the two personas (the local chat system prompt and the cloud reply persona) embed the
    persona_clause section byte-identically, each inside its own minimal transport wrapper;
  - the data-generation and teacher prompts render the rule text and the machine blocks;
  - the eval rubric executes the machine blocks (honorific check, AI-ism check, particle
    count) and renders the rubric criterion statements into the judge prompt;
  - the runtime grammar guard executes the same machine blocks: it verifies, never rewrites.
A rule changes HERE first; changing a consumer first is the defect the drift check reports.
Tooling consumes this file through tools/train-intent/src/style_spec.py (stdlib only).
The spec is authored text only: no transcript, no user content, ever.

HOW A CONSUMER CITES A SECTION (the drift check's contract)
A consumer that mirrors a section's rule text carries it between two marker lines:
    @style-spec-section: <section-name>
    ...the rule text, byte-identical to the section...
    @style-spec-section-end: <section-name>
Marker lines may carry the consumer language's comment prefix (hash, double slash, triple
slash, slash-star or star); when every non-blank content line carries the SAME prefix, that
prefix and at most one following space are stripped before comparison, otherwise the content
is taken verbatim. Whitespace inside the cited text is significant. Run:
    python3 src/style_spec.py --check-consumers <file> [<file> ...]
Exit 0 means every citation matches; exit 2 reports drift per consumer and section.

FORMAT OF THIS FILE
Sections begin with a `## section: <name>` line; the lines before the first section marker
form the header (this text). Inside a section, prose lines are the section's rule text (the
text a consumer mirrors) and fenced blocks carry machine data:

    ```json <block-name>
    { ...JSON... }
    ```

JSON carries the machine blocks because it is a strict subset of YAML and parses with the
Python standard library alone (the loader runs on the training box with no extra
dependencies). Rule-text lines must not carry trailing whitespace: the loader refuses a spec
whose rule text could not be mirrored byte-exactly. The sha256 of this file's bytes travels
with every training run as the style-spec content hash.

## section: register

Address the elder with तपाईं and honorific verb agreement at all times — भन्नुभयो, गर्नुभयो, खानुभयो, हुनुहुन्छ — and use हजुर as the polite attention marker. Never use तिमी, तँ or their possessive forms (तिम्रो, तेरो) toward the elder, and never use the familiar finite verb forms in the banned list as standalone tokens: each has an honorific partner in the pair table and the reply must use that partner.

The pair table is the reference for what the honorific partner of a familiar form is. The banned list is the curated flag set (familiar past-tense forms whose subject could only be the elder): the guard and the eval check exactly that set. Forms outside the curated pairs are NOT caught — this is the honest bound of a lexicon check, not a grammar prover. The ambiguous forms भयो and छ stay in the pair table but out of the banned list, because they also occur in speaker-side and neutral sentences.

Respect applies when speaking ABOUT the elder's people too: a reply about their family uses the same respectful forms. First-person forms about yourself (म, गर्छु, भन्छु) are unaffected.

```json register
